\details{
bbplot layers

`bb_hline()` and `bb_vline()` draw horizontal and vertical reference lines.
The position is either a constant vector (`yintercept` / `xintercept`) or a
mapping of the same name, so thresholds can come from the data as well.

By default a reference line does **not** change the axis range: a line at the
edge of the data should not silently extend the canvas. Set
`expand_limits = TRUE` when the intercept is meant to be inside the visible
range even though the data do not reach it.

A mapped intercept wins over the constant argument. With a mapped intercept,
`col` and `lty` may also be mapped and are then applied per line; with a
constant intercept those aesthetics are layer-wide constants.

When the layer brings its own data, inherited mappings are evaluated on that
data too; use `inherit = FALSE` when the plot maps aesthetics the reference
lines do not carry.

The layers follow the grammar contract: they inherit data and mapping, accept
their own, draw in `+` order, honour constant `col`, `lty` and `lwd`, and are
drawn once per facet panel.

`bb_ribbon()` draws a band between `ymin` and `ymax` along `x`, one polygon
per group. It follows the grammar contract: it inherits the plot data and
mapping, accepts its own, draws in `+` order, honours constants and
contributes `x`, `ymin` and `ymax` to the ranges.

Rows with a missing or non-finite `x`, `ymin` or `ymax` are dropped, and so
are rows with a missing group (with a warning). Within a group the points are
sorted by `x`, duplicates are kept, and a group needs at least two points to
form a polygon (smaller groups are skipped with a warning). `ymin` and `ymax`
may be given in either order: the band is drawn between them. `alpha`
(between 0 and 1), `border`, `lty` and `lwd` are layer-wide single values;
`fill` (or `col`) sets the interior and can be a constant or a mapping.

When the layer brings its own data, inherited mappings are still evaluated
on that data: give the layer the variables it inherits, or set
`inherit = FALSE` when the plot has aesthetics the band does not carry.

`bb_smooth()` fits an explicit model per group and draws the fitted curve,
optionally with a confidence band. The fit method is never implicit: choose
`"lm"` or `"loess"` (the default), pass extra fitting arguments through
`method.args`, and control the band with `se` and `level`.

`x` and `y` are mapped from the layer's data, `group` splits the fit, and a
mapped `col` colours each curve. The confidence band uses `fill` (or the
curve colour at low alpha). Small samples that cannot support the requested
fit are reported with a warning and fall back to a linear fit; a group with
fewer than two points is skipped. Fits are computed when the layer is added,
so repeated draws do not re-fit.

`bb_violin()` draws one density polygon per group: `x` selects the group
(a factor, character or numeric position), `y` is the numeric variable whose
distribution is estimated with `stats::density()`. The density is scaled to
`width` in x units, so groups stay comparable; `adjust` and `bw` control the
bandwidth, `kernel`, `trim` and `n` are passed to `density()`.

The layer follows the grammar contract: it inherits data and mapping, accepts
its own, draws in `+` order, honours constant or mapped `fill`/`col`, and
contributes the group positions and the numeric values to the ranges. Only
vertical violins are drawn. A group needs at least two finite values and a
non-zero spread; other groups are skipped with a warning. Rows with a
non-finite `y` are dropped.
}
